<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  **The doctype has to be the first thing in the file**, for the reason the Read Me pane's own
  comment gives: Installer sniffs the data, and a file starting with a comment is shown as raw
  markup. tools/platform/macos/installer-remote.sh asserts the first line on every build.

  The pane Installer shows when it has finished. @DATA_LOCATIONS@ is substituted with
  tools/platform/macos/pkg-remote/data-locations.txt, HTML-escaped.

  **That block is shared with tools/platform/macos/uninstall-remote.sh and comes from one file**,
  because the thing people fear about an uninstaller is that it takes the collection with it: the
  promise is made here and kept there, and two copies of it could disagree.
-->
<html>
<head><meta charset="utf-8"></head>
<body style="font: 13px -apple-system, sans-serif; margin: 0">

<p><b>Installed.</b></p>

<p><i>KM Remote</i> is in <b>Applications</b>. Open it and it looks for a karaoke machine on this
network by itself; if there is more than one, or if it finds none, its Setup tab is where you name
one.</p>

<p>macOS asks once for permission to find devices on the local network. That is how it finds a
machine at all. Declining it leaves the song list, the search and your favorites working &mdash;
they are answered from this Mac's own copy &mdash; and stops discovery, queueing and refreshing.</p>

<pre style="font: 12px ui-monospace, monospace">@DATA_LOCATIONS@</pre>

<p><b>To remove this later</b>, open <code>/usr/local/km-remote</code> and double-click
<b>Uninstall KM Remote</b>. It lists what it will remove and asks before it does anything. From a
terminal, <code>sudo /usr/local/km-remote/uninstall.sh</code> is the same thing.</p>

</body>
</html>
